\subsection{The Announce Request/Response Cycle: A Walkthrough}

Before diving into the HTTP implementation, let us trace through a complete
announce cycle with concrete data. This walkthrough illustrates how all the
pieces fit together.

\subsubsection{Constructing the Request}

Suppose our client is starting to download a torrent with the following
characteristics:

\begin{itemize}
\item Announce URL: \texttt{http://tracker.example.com:6969/announce}
\item Info hash (20 bytes, shown in hex): \texttt{aaf4c61d...} (SHA1 of ``hello'')
\item Our peer ID: \texttt{-LT0001-abcdefghijkl} (Literate Torrent, version 0.0.01)
\item Port: 6881 (standard BitTorrent port)
\item Torrent size: 1,073,741,824 bytes (1 GiB)
\end{itemize}

For the first announce (event=started), with nothing downloaded yet, we
construct this URL:

\begin{verbatim}
http://tracker.example.com:6969/announce
  ?info_hash=%AA%F4%C6%1D%DC%C5%E8%A2%DA%BE%DE%5BT%CAz%A4%E77%92%02
  &peer_id=-LT0001-abcdefghijkl
  &port=6881
  &uploaded=0
  &downloaded=0
  &left=1073741824
  &event=started
  &compact=1
\end{verbatim}

Note how the info hash is percent-encoded (binary bytes become \texttt{\%XX}),
while the peer ID passes through mostly unchanged since it consists of
unreserved ASCII characters.

\subsubsection{The HTTP Request}

Our client sends an HTTP/1.1 GET request:

\begin{verbatim}
GET /announce?info_hash=%AA%F4...&peer_id=...&port=6881&... HTTP/1.1
Host: tracker.example.com:6969
User-Agent: LiterateTorrent/0.1
Accept: */*
Connection: close
\end{verbatim}

The tracker receives this, looks up the info hash in its database, finds
other peers currently sharing this torrent, and prepares a response.

\subsubsection{The Tracker's Response}

The tracker responds with HTTP 200 and a bencoded dictionary body. Here is
an example response (with the bencoded structure shown in readable form):

\begin{verbatim}
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: text/plain
Content-Length: 87

d
  8:interval  i1800e
  12:min interval  i900e
  8:complete  i15e
  10:incomplete  i47e
  5:peers  30:<binary peer data: 5 peers x 6 bytes each>
e
\end{verbatim}

The actual bencoded response is a continuous byte stream without whitespace.
The peer data in compact format looks like this (30 bytes for 5 peers):

\begin{verbatim}
Offset   Bytes (hex)         Decoded
------   ------------------  -------------------------
0-5      C0 A8 01 0A 1A E1   192.168.1.10:6881
6-11     0A 00 00 01 1F 90   10.0.0.1:8080
12-17    5D B8 D8 22 16 88   93.184.216.34:5768
18-23    AC 10 00 05 0B B8   172.16.0.5:3000
24-29    2F 68 9C A1 C8 01   47.104.156.161:51201
\end{verbatim}

\subsubsection{Parsing the Response}

Our client parses the bencoded response and extracts:

\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{interval}: 1800 seconds (30 minutes until next regular announce)
\item \texttt{min interval}: 900 seconds (minimum 15 minutes between any announces)
\item \texttt{complete}: 15 seeders have the complete file
\item \texttt{incomplete}: 47 leechers are still downloading
\item \texttt{peers}: 5 peer addresses to connect to
\end{itemize}

Armed with this information, our client can now establish TCP connections
to the five peers and begin exchanging piece data. After 30 minutes (or sooner
if needed), we will announce again---but without the \texttt{event} parameter,
since it is only used for lifecycle events.

\subsubsection{Error Responses}

If the tracker rejects our request, it returns a bencoded dictionary with
a \texttt{failure reason} key instead of the normal fields:

\begin{verbatim}
d14:failure reason22:torrent not registerede
\end{verbatim}

This decodes to: \texttt{\{``failure reason'': ``torrent not registered''\}}.
Our client must detect this failure response before attempting to extract
peer data.

\subsection{HTTP Announce Request}

With the announce URL constructed, we now need to actually send the HTTP request
to the tracker and retrieve the response. This is the network boundary of our
tracker module---the point where our carefully assembled URL meets the real world
of network latency, DNS failures, and uncooperative servers.

The HTTP request lifecycle for a tracker announce is straightforward:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Build the HTTP client}: We configure a reqwest client with our
  desired settings: a 30-second timeout (per the PRD), a User-Agent header
  identifying our client, and redirect following enabled.

\item \textbf{Send the GET request}: The tracker protocol uses HTTP GET with all
  parameters in the query string. We send the request and await the response.

\item \textbf{Handle errors}: Network errors (connection refused, DNS failure,
  timeouts) are converted to our [[TrackerError]] type. We specifically distinguish
  timeouts from other errors to enable smarter retry logic.

\item \textbf{Check the status code}: HTTP 200 indicates success. Any other status
  code (4xx client errors, 5xx server errors) is treated as an error, though the
  response body may still contain useful diagnostic information.

\item \textbf{Return the response body}: On success, we return the raw bytes of
  the response body. The caller is responsible for parsing this as bencoded data
  and extracting the peer list.
\end{enumerate}

The 30-second timeout may seem long, but tracker servers are often overloaded,
especially for popular torrents. A shorter timeout would cause unnecessary
failures during peak usage. The timeout covers the entire request---connection
establishment, TLS handshake, sending the request, and receiving the response.

<<http request>>=
use std::time::Duration;

/// The User-Agent string sent with tracker requests.
///
/// This identifies our client to the tracker, useful for debugging and
/// statistics. The format follows HTTP conventions: product/version.
pub const USER_AGENT: &str = "LiterateTorrent/0.1";

/// Timeout for tracker HTTP requests, in seconds.
///
/// This is 30 seconds as specified in the PRD. The timeout covers the entire
/// HTTP request lifecycle: DNS resolution, TCP connection, TLS handshake,
/// sending the request, and receiving the response.
pub const TRACKER_TIMEOUT_SECS: u64 = 30;

/// Send an HTTP GET request to a tracker and return the response body.
///
/// This function sends the fully-constructed announce URL to the tracker and
/// returns the raw response bytes. The caller is responsible for parsing the
/// bencoded response.
///
/// # Arguments
///
/// * `url` - The complete announce URL with all query parameters
///
/// # Returns
///
/// On success, returns the response body as a byte vector. The body is typically
/// a bencoded dictionary containing the peer list and announce interval.
///
/// # Errors
///
/// Returns [`TrackerError::TrackerTimeout`] if the request times out after 30 seconds.
///
/// Returns [`TrackerError::TrackerHttpError`] for other HTTP failures:
/// - Connection refused or reset
/// - DNS resolution failure
/// - TLS/SSL errors
/// - HTTP 4xx or 5xx status codes
///
/// # Example
///
/// ```ignore
/// let url = build_announce_url(&announce_url, &request);
/// match announce_http(&url).await {
///     Ok(body) => { /* parse bencoded response */ }
///     Err(TrackerError::TrackerTimeout) => { /* try another tracker */ }
///     Err(e) => { /* log and retry with backoff */ }
/// }
/// ```
pub async fn announce_http(url: &str) -> Result<Vec<u8>, TrackerError> {
    let client = reqwest::Client::builder()
        .timeout(Duration::from_secs(TRACKER_TIMEOUT_SECS))
        .user_agent(USER_AGENT)
        // Redirects are enabled by default in reqwest, but we explicitly
        // acknowledge this since some trackers use redirects for load balancing
        .redirect(reqwest::redirect::Policy::default())
        .build()
        .map_err(|e| {
            // Client build errors are rare (bad TLS config, etc.)
            // Treat them as HTTP errors since they prevent communication
            TrackerError::TrackerHttpError(e)
        })?;

    let response = client
        .get(url)
        .send()
        .await
        .map_err(|e| {
            // Check if this was a timeout
            if e.is_timeout() {
                TrackerError::TrackerTimeout
            } else {
                TrackerError::TrackerHttpError(e)
            }
        })?;

    // Check for HTTP error status codes
    // reqwest's error_for_status() converts 4xx/5xx to errors
    let response = response.error_for_status().map_err(|e| {
        // Status errors are HTTP-level failures
        TrackerError::TrackerHttpError(e)
    })?;

    // Read the response body
    let body = response.bytes().await.map_err(|e| {
        // Body read errors could be timeouts or connection resets
        if e.is_timeout() {
            TrackerError::TrackerTimeout
        } else {
            TrackerError::TrackerHttpError(e)
        }
    })?;

    Ok(body.to_vec())
}
@

The implementation uses reqwest's builder pattern to configure the HTTP client.
Key design decisions:

\begin{description}
\item[Client per request] We create a new client for each request rather than
  reusing one. This is slightly less efficient but simpler and avoids lifetime
  complexity. For a BitTorrent client that announces every 30+ minutes, the
  overhead is negligible.

\item[Timeout covers everything] The reqwest timeout applies to the entire request,
  not just individual operations. This matches the PRD specification and ensures
  we don't hang indefinitely on slow trackers.

\item[Explicit timeout error] We check [[is_timeout()]] on reqwest errors and
  convert them to [[TrackerTimeout]]. This allows the caller to distinguish
  ``tracker is slow'' from ``tracker is broken.''

\item[User-Agent identification] We set a User-Agent header that identifies our
  client. Some trackers block requests without a User-Agent, and it helps tracker
  operators debug issues. The format follows HTTP conventions.

\item[Redirect following] Reqwest follows redirects by default (up to 10 hops).
  We explicitly acknowledge this because some trackers use HTTP redirects for
  load balancing or to route clients to geographic-specific servers.
\end{description}

\subsection{HTTP Request Tests}

Testing the HTTP request function is challenging because it involves real network
operations. We focus on testing the error mapping logic, which we can verify
without making actual HTTP requests.

