\subsection{Announce Intervals and the Re-Announce Cycle}

A BitTorrent client does not announce just once---it must periodically
re-announce to the tracker throughout the download lifecycle. This serves
several purposes:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Keep-alive}: The tracker needs to know we are still active.
  Without periodic announcements, the tracker would eventually remove us from
  its peer list (typically after 30--60 minutes of inactivity).

\item \textbf{Statistics update}: Each announce reports our current upload/download
  statistics. Trackers use this for ratio enforcement (on private trackers) and
  for displaying swarm statistics.

\item \textbf{Fresh peers}: The tracker may return different peers on each
  announce, giving us opportunities to connect to faster or more reliable peers.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Timing Parameters}

The tracker controls announce timing through two response fields:

\begin{description}
\item[interval] The number of seconds until the next \emph{regular} announce.
  This is mandatory in every successful response. Typical values range from
  300 seconds (5 minutes) for small trackers to 3600 seconds (1 hour) for
  high-traffic trackers like those serving Linux distributions.

\item[min interval] The minimum number of seconds between \emph{any} two
  announces. This is optional but, when present, acts as a hard limit. The
  client must not announce more frequently than this, even for event-driven
  announces like ``completed.''
\end{description}

These timing parameters serve as a load-balancing mechanism. A tracker serving
millions of peers would be overwhelmed if every client announced every minute.
By specifying longer intervals, the tracker can reduce load while still
maintaining reasonably current peer lists.

\subsubsection{The Re-Announce State Machine}

Our client's announce behavior follows this state machine:

\begin{verbatim}
                  +-------------+
                  |   INITIAL   |
                  +------+------+
                         |
                         | (startup)
                         v
           +-------------+-------------+
           |  ANNOUNCE(event=started)  |
           +-------------+-------------+
                         |
                         | (wait interval seconds)
                         v
           +-------------+-------------+
           |   ANNOUNCE(event=none)    |<--------+
           +-------------+-------------+         |
                         |                       |
                         | (wait interval)       |
                         +-----------------------+
                         |
                         | (download complete)
                         v
           +-------------+-------------+
           |  ANNOUNCE(event=completed)|
           +-------------+-------------+
                         |
                         | (user quits)
                         v
           +-------------+-------------+
           |  ANNOUNCE(event=stopped)  |
           +-------------+-------------+
                         |
                         v
                  +-------------+
                  |    DONE     |
                  +-------------+
\end{verbatim}

Key transitions:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{First announce}: Always includes \texttt{event=started}. This
  registers us with the tracker.

\item \textbf{Periodic announces}: Omit the event parameter. These occur
  every \texttt{interval} seconds as specified by the tracker.

\item \textbf{Completion announce}: When the download finishes (all pieces
  verified), we send \texttt{event=completed} exactly once. This tells the
  tracker we have transitioned from leecher to seeder.

\item \textbf{Shutdown announce}: When the client exits cleanly, we send
  \texttt{event=stopped}. This allows the tracker to remove us immediately
  rather than waiting for our entry to expire.
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Handling Interval Changes}

The tracker may change the interval between announces. Our client should
always use the most recent interval value. For example:

\begin{itemize}
\item First response: \texttt{interval=1800} (30 minutes)
\item Second response: \texttt{interval=3600} (1 hour)---tracker is under load
\item Third response: \texttt{interval=900} (15 minutes)---load reduced
\end{itemize}

This dynamic adjustment allows trackers to respond to load conditions. During
peak hours or DDoS attacks, trackers may dramatically increase intervals to
survive.

\subsubsection{Failure Recovery}

When an announce fails (timeout, HTTP error, tracker error response), the
client should:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Log the failure for debugging.
\item Wait a brief period (e.g., 30 seconds to 2 minutes) before retrying.
\item If using announce-list (multiple trackers), try the next tracker in
  the list.
\item Continue with existing peer connections---they are not affected by
  tracker failures.
\end{enumerate}

A single failed announce is not catastrophic. The client can continue
downloading with its existing peers. However, prolonged tracker unavailability
prevents new peers from discovering us and vice versa.

\subsection{Limitations of Tracker-Based Discovery}

While tracker-based peer discovery is simple and widely supported, it has
significant limitations that have motivated the development of alternative
discovery mechanisms.

\subsubsection{Single Point of Failure}

The tracker is a centralized service. If the tracker goes offline:

\begin{itemize}
\item New peers cannot join the swarm---they have no way to discover existing
  participants.
\item Existing peers can continue exchanging data with their current connections,
  but cannot find new peers.
\item If the torrent's only tracker becomes permanently unavailable, the swarm
  eventually dies as peers disconnect and cannot be replaced.
\end{itemize}

The \texttt{announce-list} extension (BEP 12) partially mitigates this by
allowing multiple backup trackers, but all listed trackers could still fail
simultaneously.

\subsubsection{Privacy Concerns}

Trackers necessarily see:

\begin{itemize}
\item Every participant's IP address
\item What torrents each IP is downloading
\item Transfer statistics (upload/download amounts)
\item How long each peer participates
\end{itemize}

This information is valuable for surveillance, copyright enforcement, or
traffic analysis. Even well-intentioned tracker operators may be compelled
to provide logs, or their systems may be compromised.

\subsubsection{Scalability Limits}

A single tracker serving a very popular torrent (e.g., a new Linux release)
must handle millions of announce requests. Even with long intervals, this
creates significant load:

\begin{verbatim}
1,000,000 peers * 1 announce / 30 minutes = 555 announces/second
\end{verbatim}

Tracker operators must invest in robust infrastructure, and even then may
struggle during peak periods.

\subsubsection{Legal and Political Vulnerability}

Tracker operators are visible targets. They can be:

\begin{itemize}
\item Served with takedown notices
\item Subjected to DDoS attacks
\item Pressured to block certain torrents
\item Prosecuted in some jurisdictions
\end{itemize}

This has led to the shutdown of many public trackers over the years.

\subsubsection{The Case for DHT}

These limitations motivated the development of the Distributed Hash Table (DHT)
protocol for BitTorrent, specified in BEP 5. DHT eliminates the central tracker
by distributing peer discovery across all participating clients:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{No single point of failure}: The network is fully decentralized.
\item \textbf{Privacy improvement}: No single entity sees all traffic.
\item \textbf{Resilience}: The network heals itself as nodes join and leave.
\end{itemize}

However, DHT is more complex to implement and has its own tradeoffs (bootstrap
problems, slower peer discovery, Sybil attack vulnerability). Our client does
not implement DHT---we rely solely on tracker-based discovery---but understanding
why DHT exists helps explain the broader BitTorrent ecosystem.

For a practical client, tracker-based discovery remains valuable even when DHT
is available. Trackers provide faster initial peer discovery, and many private
trackers (which require registration and enforce ratio) do not allow DHT at all.
